        "title": "Question 2: Article for Publication",
        "prompt": "Write an article for publication in a national daily newspaper on the topic: \"The Menace of Plastic Pollution in Marine Waters and Practical Solutions to Safeguard Ghana's Coastal Ecosystem.\"",
        "markingScheme": "RESCUING OUR OCEANS: TACKLING MARINE PLASTIC POLLUTION IN GHANA\nBy Samuel K. Boateng, Begoro\n\nGhana's 550-kilometer Atlantic coastline is endowed with productive fisheries, scenic sandy beaches, and vital coastal lagoons that support millions of livelihoods. However, an alarming ecological catastrophe is systematically suffocating our marine habitat: the unchecked proliferation of single-use plastic waste dumped into coastal waters.\n\nThe primary catalyst of this crisis is the improper disposal of non-biodegradable synthetic plastics in urban centers. Millions of single-use polythene bags, plastic bottles, and water sachets discarded on city streets wash into open drainage channels during torrential downpours, discharging directly into coastal estuaries and the Atlantic Ocean. Because petroleum-based plastics require centuries to decompose, they accumulate on beaches, transforming pristine coastlines into repulsive rubbish dumps that deter tourism and destroy local economies.\n\nMore disastrously, marine plastic pollution devastates aquatic fauna and contaminates the human food chain. Marine creatures like sea turtles mistake floating translucent plastic bags for jellyfish, ingesting them and dying from intestinal blockage. Over time, wave turbulence and solar radiation shatter plastic waste into microscopic fragments known as microplastics. These toxic particles are ingested by sardines and commercial fish species, bio-accumulating in the tissues of fish that human families consume, exposing consumers to severe carcinogens and cellular toxicity.\n\nTo safeguard our coastal ecosystem, the central government must enact comprehensive legislation banning single-use non-essential polythene bags, while subsidizing eco-friendly biodegradable alternatives produced from cassava starch. Secondly, municipal assemblies must establish coastal recycling buy-back centers that pay artisanal fishermen for retrieving plastics from the sea.\n\nOur oceans are our sacred heritage; we must clean our waters before marine life vanishes forever.",
