        "title": "Question 3: Narrative Moral Story",
        "prompt": "Write an engaging, realistic story that illustrates the traditional proverb: \"A bird in the hand is worth two in the bush.\"",
        "markingScheme": "A BIRD IN THE HAND IS WORTH TWO IN THE BUSH\n\nDuring our final vacation before entering junior high school, my close friend, Kweku, was offered a remarkable apprenticeship opportunity. A respected master carpenter in our village, Opanyin Boateng, offered to take Kweku into his workshop, paying him a modest monthly allowance of three hundred cedis while training him in modern furniture joinery. Opanyin Boateng promised that upon completing basic school, Kweku would be fully certified as a master joiner.\n\nI encouraged Kweku to embrace the opportunity with open arms. However, Kweku's greedy cousin, Patrick, arrived from the commercial capital with wild, dazzling stories of quick riches. Patrick claimed that if Kweku traveled with him to the city, an elusive foreign businessman would hire him to manage a luxury electronics boutique for two thousand cedis monthly. Dazzled by the prospect of instant wealth, Kweku rejected Opanyin Boateng's modest apprenticeship with disdain, laughing mockingly: \"Why should I sweep sawdust for a meager three hundred cedis when a fortune awaits me in Accra?\" I cautioned him that \"a bird in the hand is worth two in the bush,\" but Kweku packed his bags and departed.\n\nThe fantasy collapsed into a nightmare. Arriving in Accra, Kweku discovered that the wealthy businessman was a fictitious phantom invented by his cousin. Patrick abandoned him in a squalid slum, penniless and without accommodation. For three months, Kweku slept on cardboard sheets outside market stalls, surviving on scraps of bread and washing dishes at chop-bars to avoid starvation.\n\nMeanwhile, Opanyin Boateng took on another humble apprentice who mastered the craft, manufactured beautiful desks, and earned substantial income. When Kweku finally returned to the village, ragged and broken by suffering, he wept bitter tears of regret. Looking at his blistered hands, the ancient truth echoed in his heart: Truly, a bird in the hand is worth two in the bush.",
        "sampleAnswer": "A BIRD IN THE HAND IS WORTH TWO IN THE BUSH\n\nDuring our final vacation before entering junior high school, my close friend, Kweku, was offered a remarkable apprenticeship opportunity. A respected master carpenter in our village, Opanyin Boateng, offered to take Kweku into his workshop, paying him a modest monthly allowance of three hundred cedis while training him in modern furniture joinery. Opanyin Boateng promised that upon completing basic school, Kweku would be fully certified as a master joiner.\n\nI encouraged Kweku to embrace the opportunity with open arms. However, Kweku's greedy cousin, Patrick, arrived from the commercial capital with wild, dazzling stories of quick riches. Patrick claimed that if Kweku traveled with him to the city, an elusive foreign businessman would hire him to manage a luxury electronics boutique for two thousand cedis monthly. Dazzled by the prospect of instant wealth, Kweku rejected Opanyin Boateng's modest apprenticeship with disdain, laughing mockingly: \"Why should I sweep sawdust for a meager three hundred cedis when a fortune awaits me in Accra?\" I cautioned him that \"a bird in the hand is worth two in the bush,\" but Kweku packed his bags and departed.\n\nThe fantasy collapsed into a nightmare. Arriving in Accra, Kweku discovered that the wealthy businessman was a fictitious phantom invented by his cousin. Patrick abandoned him in a squalid slum, penniless and without accommodation. For three months, Kweku slept on cardboard sheets outside market stalls, surviving on scraps of bread and washing dishes at chop-bars to avoid starvation.\n\nMeanwhile, Opanyin Boateng took on another humble apprentice who mastered the craft, manufactured beautiful desks, and earned substantial income. When Kweku finally returned to the village, ragged and broken by suffering, he wept bitter tears of regret. Looking at his blistered hands, the ancient truth echoed in his heart: Truly, a bird in the hand is worth two in the bush.",
        "workedSolution": "A BIRD IN THE HAND IS WORTH TWO IN THE BUSH\n\nDuring our final vacation before entering junior high school, my close friend, Kweku, was offered a remarkable apprenticeship opportunity. A respected master carpenter in our village, Opanyin Boateng, offered to take Kweku into his workshop, paying him a modest monthly allowance of three hundred cedis while training him in modern furniture joinery. Opanyin Boateng promised that upon completing basic school, Kweku would be fully certified as a master joiner.\n\nI encouraged Kweku to embrace the opportunity with open arms. However, Kweku's greedy cousin, Patrick, arrived from the commercial capital with wild, dazzling stories of quick riches. Patrick claimed that if Kweku traveled with him to the city, an elusive foreign businessman would hire him to manage a luxury electronics boutique for two thousand cedis monthly. Dazzled by the prospect of instant wealth, Kweku rejected Opanyin Boateng's modest apprenticeship with disdain, laughing mockingly: \"Why should I sweep sawdust for a meager three hundred cedis when a fortune awaits me in Accra?\" I cautioned him that \"a bird in the hand is worth two in the bush,\" but Kweku packed his bags and departed.\n\nThe fantasy collapsed into a nightmare. Arriving in Accra, Kweku discovered that the wealthy businessman was a fictitious phantom invented by his cousin. Patrick abandoned him in a squalid slum, penniless and without accommodation. For three months, Kweku slept on cardboard sheets outside market stalls, surviving on scraps of bread and washing dishes at chop-bars to avoid starvation.\n\nMeanwhile, Opanyin Boateng took on another humble apprentice who mastered the craft, manufactured beautiful desks, and earned substantial income. When Kweku finally returned to the village, ragged and broken by suffering, he wept bitter tears of regret. Looking at his blistered hands, the ancient truth echoed in his heart: Truly, a bird in the hand is worth two in the bush.",
